=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Atlantic Salmon have 29 haploid chromosomes.
  - What fraction of the gametes from a triploid male salmon would be euploid, having exactly one copy of each chromosome (1 of each chromosome)?
- If a male triploid salmon produces 1 billion sperm, how many of these will be euploid?

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Atlantic Salmon have 29 haploid chromosomes. If a male triploid salmon produces 1 billion sperm, how many of these will be euploid?
  - Each chromosome has a &#189; chance of being euploid (segregating correctly into the gamete).
  - For 29 chromosomes, the probability of all being correct is\:
    - &#189; to the 29th power\: (1/2)29 = 1/536,870,912
    - This means only 1 in 536,870,912 sperm will be euploid.
  - The expected number of euploid sperm is\:
    - 1,000,000,000 / 536,870,912 = 1.86 sperm
- So, for Atlantic Salmon, [*only about 2 sperm out of 1 billion*]{color=red} will be euploid.
  - Despite this extreme inefficiency, salmon produce an enormous number of sperm. Even a tiny fraction of functional gametes could theoretically fertilize an egg

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Polyploids have more than two complete chromosome sets.
- Odd chromosome sets complicate pairing during meiosis.
- Chromosome doubling can restore pairing in a hybrid.
- Plant breeding uses polyploidy to change crop traits.

